WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.420
Nature shows itself to us as a lion's tail, but I have no doubt that there is the lion himself too,

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:14.190
although because of his size he cannot appear to us all at once.

3
00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:18.990
We can see him only from the point of view of a flea sitting on him.

4
00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:25.640
This was said by Albert Einstein, and I wouldn't add anything here.

5
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:31.240
After everything, we all need this return to nature.

6
00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:40.740
A return to ourselves, a return to other people.

7
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:48.380
A return to the life we had before.

8
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.670
We all want things to go back to normal.

9
00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:58.890
So we started from the beginning. With nature.

10
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:06.290
This is the story of a completely unusually ordinary weekend in Neverland.

11
00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:11.370
Welcome to a new video.

12
00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.950
We begin our journey at the Ram Fortress.

13
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.370
From the Ram Fortress we'll cross by ferry to the other bank.

14
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:24.030
It's a good option if, say, you were at Silver Lake or went to the Golubac Fortress,

15
00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.190
and want to get to, say, the Deliblato Sands.

16
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.990
We're going on this trip as a whole family.

17
00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:30.990
And there's the dog, this puppy that's barking — that's Kenny.

18
00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.870
We went for 4 days, but it's as if for 74.

19
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.950
I can't describe how loaded we are.

20
00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.850
I don't know if I'll even be able to open the trunk without things falling out.

21
00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:41.630
I think I won't.

22
00:01:57.790 --> 00:03:35.400
I don't know about you, but this is exactly how I pictured grandma's little house from that famous fairy tale.

23
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.190
Nadja, dad will tell it to you now.

24
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.560
Once upon a time there was a girl who, because of her red hood, was called Little Red Riding Hood.

25
00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:56.790
One day she set off to her grandmother, who lived in the forest.

26
00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.530
Little Red Riding Hood knew that a charming wolf lived in this forest.

27
00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:07.870
The wolf knew where Little Red Riding Hood was going, and took a shortcut to reach grandma's house before her.

28
00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:14.050
We settled in, had a small snack and set off for a walk right away.

29
00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:20.030
Slavica, our host and guide, was immediately ready for action.

30
00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:24.570
She gave everyone a stick, that is, a pole.

31
00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:27.750
It should help us when walking.

32
00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:32.570
We're going on a hike, she says, we'll be gone for 2–3 hours.

33
00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.450
And so we all set off together, from the youngest to the oldest.

34
00:04:36.810 --> 00:04:39.010
All five of us, counting Kenny — six.

35
00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.810
And Slavica with us, so there are seven of us.

36
00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.950
The first thing I noticed just now, when I came out of the cabin, that is, the little house —

37
00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:49.910
is that I can't hear anything.

38
00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.130
I can't hear anything, such silence.

39
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.370
What silence, folks.

40
00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.830
This cabin is surrounded by a coniferous forest.

41
00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:11.930
Okay, there are other species here too, but let's say conifers predominate here.

42
00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.430
I can already see this will be a weekend we'll remember.

43
00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:17.970
And here are just a couple of reasons why.

44
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.790
There's no TV here, no internet, people here are forced to be with each other,

45
00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:25.530
and that's the point of this whole story.

46
00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:32.530
Enjoying time with loved ones, talking, listening — that's all.

47
00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:33.290
That's it.

48
00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:36.810
It's what all of us are missing after this corona.

49
00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:37.310
Isn't it?

50
00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:39.250
Let's go.

51
00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:56.330
I'm going back to the cabin now to get our stroller.

52
00:05:56.810 --> 00:05:58.430
We didn't know the situation here was like this.

53
00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:02.430
In principle, the stroller can be pushed very well along this road.

54
00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.910
Even though it goes through the forest, it can be done.

55
00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:06.290
There, you see.

56
00:06:07.330 --> 00:06:09.110
Well, yes, there are small bumps.

57
00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.490
After all, it's a forest road.

58
00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:11.910
But it can be done, it can.

59
00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:14.190
Now we'll put Nadja in the stroller

60
00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:16.710
and we'll definitely move much faster.

61
00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:19.090
This reminds me...

62
00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.110
I don't know if I ever told you, but

63
00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:23.410
I was even in the scouts.

64
00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:24.650
Not for long.

65
00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:29.130
Let's say I had two or three such trips.

66
00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:35.010
This is, let's say, the boundary.

67
00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.390
A fenced-off part, and there are hunting grounds there.

68
00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.410
We're walking along the hunting grounds.

69
00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.050
I learned that naturalists come here.

70
00:06:42.230 --> 00:06:43.370
People who watch birds.

71
00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:45.350
People who watch insects.

72
00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:48.050
People who watch trees.

73
00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.190
Behind me is the old riverbed of the Karaš.

74
00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:54.990
By the way, the Karaš is a river that enters Serbia from Romania.

75
00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:56.650
Once again I say: silence.

76
00:06:57.710 --> 00:06:59.770
It's broken only by one tractor and Kenny.

77
00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:01.370
Otherwise, folks, what peace.

78
00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.010
There was one heron in the shot.

79
00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:07.510
I tried to film it, but it flew away.

80
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.450
But we'll see each other again.

81
00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.250
Come on, home. Home! Home, quick!

82
00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:26.220
Sheep...

83
00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.800
The team went a little ahead.

84
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.800
I stopped to show you...

85
00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:37.980
I wanted to show you: this plant.

86
00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.920
It's a flower called "saša."

87
00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.000
And I tell Slavica that it's not foreign to me.

88
00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.780
Everyone calls me that, let's say, in other countries.

89
00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.300
Greeks, Turks, I don't know...

90
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.220
That's what they called me in Thailand.

91
00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.220
So yes, saša.

92
00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.100
This plant, that is, this flower, grows only here.

93
00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:57.120
It's nowhere else.

94
00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.260
This is the famous buttercup.

95
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.480
And this is my stick that I'm leaning on now.

96
00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:04.520
Because we're walking through the steppe.

97
00:08:06.820 --> 00:08:07.400
Juniper.

98
00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:13.220
The berries of this tree are used to make the widely known klekovača.

99
00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.440
Juniper is great for people who have respiratory problems.

100
00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:25.180
They recommend standing about 15–20 centimeters from the plant and breathing.

101
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:32.160
And they say that after a while you can say goodbye to your inhaler, if you were using one before.

102
00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:39.690
The stick is in place.

103
00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:41.670
On we go.

104
00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.460
We're still walking

105
00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.380
through the Deliblato Sands.

106
00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:55.740
These are marked roads, marked trails.

107
00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:57.160
And it's very beautiful here.

108
00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.360
Beautiful, really.

109
00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.000
Real nature, that very "Serbian Tuscany," as these parts are called.

110
00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:08.420
Tomorrow, if all goes to plan, we'll see the Zagajica Hills too.

111
00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:14.140
I think this weekend we'll truly relax and have a good rest.

112
00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:19.040
Because there really is no internet or TV here.

113
00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.520
Everything is green here already.

114
00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.720
And here there really is everything you could want.

115
00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.740
The dog is enjoying it, but he's already on his last legs.

116
00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:33.080
He can't walk anymore.

117
00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:34.500
Because he's really not used to it.

118
00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.040
Nadja is asleep.

119
00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:44.140
My parents are keeping up the pace.

120
00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:49.900
So far the family walk is going well.

121
00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:58.300
Trips where there are very few people over a huge area

122
00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.700
will be exceptionally popular.

123
00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:08.440
Of course, the focus will be mainly on nature and on places

124
00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:12.520
that are not yet so popular and explored.

125
00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:16.840
And one of them in Serbia is the Deliblato Sands,

126
00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.400
where we are now.

127
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:21.580
In front of us is a well.

128
00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.220
I'll go and film it for you now.

129
00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.730
This is actually anti-stress therapy.

130
00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:27.690
Mom, you can stroke the moss.

131
00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:36.690
Good morning, folks.

132
00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:40.150
Our new day here in the Deliblato Sands.

133
00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.630
It's about half past nine.

134
00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:45.170
We all got up at nine.

135
00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.130
We fell asleep around half past ten.

136
00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.830
Which means we literally all passed out.

137
00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.690
I think the walk of ten kilometers yesterday

138
00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:54.970
is to blame.

139
00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:56.450
I still haven't woken up.

140
00:11:57.050 --> 00:11:57.850
Breakfast first.

141
00:11:59.130 --> 00:11:59.950
Breakfast is ready.

142
00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:01.130
And coffee.

143
00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.690
What a wonderful feeling when you're woken by birdsong.

144
00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.770
The air is fresh here, there's always a breeze blowing.

145
00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.690
You can hear the wind passing through the pine needles.

146
00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:22.630
I don't know if you can hear these birds.

147
00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:23.650
Listen closely.

148
00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:28.250
Besides the birds, you'll hear a serious, serious silence.

149
00:12:54.320 --> 00:13:00.280
You can actually go on foot, but we decided to go by car after all.

150
00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:03.300
So now let's see what it costs us.

151
00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:04.400
"Lada Niva."

152
00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.140
We need a "Lada Niva."

153
00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:13.060
We have about 2.8 km left, or 13 minutes, which is really a lot,

154
00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:15.700
considering how many minutes for two and a half kilometers, but oh well.

155
00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.260
Before that it wasn't so bad.

156
00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:28.160
It's a dirt road laid by tractors, and the ruts are quite deep.

157
00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:30.180
So it's okay, slowly.

158
00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:32.140
The good thing — we're not alone.

159
00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:34.500
There are other cars both behind us and in front of us.

160
00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:36.820
A small convoy, and slowly.

161
00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:40.940
There, I'm following this couple ahead of us, who were the first to take this route.

162
00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.680
I'm behind them, and then we'll see.

163
00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:46.800
The good thing — we're already used to roads like this.

164
00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.100
And for us this is, let's say, nothing unusual.

165
00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:04.800
In any case, you can walk here.

166
00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:14.660
That's exactly what people usually end up deciding to do, if you're really not a very skilled driver.

167
00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:16.440
They all say it's worth it.

168
00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:21.620
So we decided to go this time without little Nadja.

169
00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:40.610
The road is a disaster. I recommend walking the last three kilometers.

170
00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:42.170
Absolutely not by car.

171
00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.670
You probably can't hear anything because of this wind.

172
00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.600
There it is, the highest peak.

173
00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.880
We've reached the very end. We'll stay here a short while

174
00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:57.480
and go back to a place that, as we saw, is very nice for photos.

175
00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:00.120
For a picnic.

176
00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:49.740
We have dust in our mouths.

177
00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:12.190
We did everything here that we planned.

178
00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:14.150
Enjoyed nature a bit.

179
00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:16.530
We chose one...

180
00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.230
Now I'll show you how good a spot it is.

181
00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:20.670
Such a view.

182
00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.640
There's nobody around us. Nowhere nearby.

183
00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:27.920
This grass was burned.

184
00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.580
That is, it was set on fire, and now our shoes and everything else look like this.

185
00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:37.110
Maja's sneakers.

186
00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:31.800
We set off on today's expedition.

187
00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:35.060
We're going back to that old riverbed of the Karaš.

188
00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:39.200
And we'll walk to a small pond that should be interesting.

189
00:17:39.320 --> 00:17:41.160
Slavica isn't with us today.

190
00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:43.100
She explained the way to us roughly.

191
00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.240
So I'm leading the team.

192
00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:47.320
With us is Srđan, who will operate the drone.

193
00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.260
And we hope we won't lose it, because the wind is blowing.

194
00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:54.520
Maja is repacking the things for the tenth time.

195
00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.000
You came with us, right?

196
00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:57.720
So you're coming with us after all, huh?

197
00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.780
We walked literally half the forest

198
00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:04.680
and only now realized that she had apparently followed us.

199
00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.660
I think she knows the way if we get lost.

200
00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:10.800
Čupka definitely won't get lost.

201
00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:21.720
There's our goal.

202
00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.660
On the other side you should be able to see the Ram Fortress,

203
00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:28.960
the Romanian Carpathians, the Đerdap Gorge and everything else.

204
00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.460
So now we're going around along this road.

205
00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.240
Come on, Čupka, let's go!

206
00:18:55.960 --> 00:19:00.420
We are now here, maybe about two kilometers from our lodging.

207
00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:03.260
There is the old arm of the Karaš.

208
00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:06.340
Not far from here, about a kilometer and a half from this place,

209
00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:08.560
we'd reach the Danube.

210
00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.880
Literally on the other side is the Ram Fortress

211
00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:14.380
and the ferry on which we crossed.

212
00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.160
We're here for one more day.

213
00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:21.420
I think it's much better to come here, say, in May

214
00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.060
or maybe in June, when everything turns green.

215
00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:25.760
For now everything here is somehow yellow.

216
00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.100
I believe it's much, much more beautiful when everything turns green.

217
00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:37.000
Čupka is with us, we're with these sticks, and says

218
00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:39.860
one fisherman, such a gloomy one, fishing here, and says:

219
00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:41.800
all you're missing is sheep.

220
00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:51.280
Good morning.

221
00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:52.820
Our last day here.

222
00:19:53.260 --> 00:19:54.960
It's about half past eight in the morning.

223
00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:57.880
The birds are singing so loudly.

224
00:19:58.480 --> 00:19:59.160
The sun is shining.

225
00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:00.900
A real natural

226
00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:02.700
idyll, literally.

227
00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:04.500
Today we're going back.

228
00:20:04.700 --> 00:20:07.080
We were here three nights, that is, four days,

229
00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.760
and I think we, perhaps, didn't get quite enough.

230
00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:12.160
We're in the forest, so here and there some beetle shows up.

231
00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:16.660
Slowly heading back to the cabin so I can finish my coffee.

232
00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:20.500
Then Maja and I will drive to see something we couldn't before,

233
00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:25.780
and try to find the man with the sheep you've already seen,

234
00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:30.720
so that Nadja can see the sheep, because she loves animals very much and imitates them wonderfully.

235
00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.120
So I think it would be great for her to see them.

236
00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:37.880
We drove to this old sweep well to take photos, and Nadja with us.

237
00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:44.200
But she fell asleep somewhere halfway, and the whole drive from our lodging

238
00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:47.100
to this sweep well takes three and a half minutes.

239
00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:49.300
Maja went off alone to photograph and film something.

240
00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:55.400
This trip was very important for both me and Maja.

241
00:20:55.520 --> 00:20:59.600
Many very beautiful moments and instants.

242
00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:05.480
But there were also some that were very sad.

243
00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:10.620
So this trip came at just the right time for us,

244
00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:13.840
to disconnect a bit.

245
00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:18.340
Maja asked me: how do you feel now without the internet?

246
00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:21.260
And I said I feel very good.

247
00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:23.180
Amazingly, I really do feel good.

248
00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:28.740
I thought I'd be twitchy, that I'd be looking all the time for my phone, and, I don't know,

249
00:21:28.740 --> 00:21:32.420
climbing trees to catch a signal.

250
00:21:32.680 --> 00:21:37.200
But I didn't do that, really, and this disconnecting did me a lot of good.

251
00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:11.120
This ferry that runs from Ram costs 1200 dinars for one car and all the passengers in it.

252
00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:15.640
There's also a set schedule for when it runs, and by months, note.

253
00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:17.800
I'll show you the schedule here now.

254
00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:22.640
In principle, they stick to it, unless the weather is really bad.

255
00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:27.540
We're slowly crossing to the other bank, heading home, and I think that's the end of our video.

256
00:22:28.180 --> 00:22:30.460
Or maybe I should finish the fairy tale for you too.

257
00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:31.560
That would be right.

258
00:22:35.780 --> 00:22:40.200
Grandma wasn't at home, but the cunning wolf knew where the key was.

259
00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.280
He entered the little house and began to wait.

260
00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:48.620
Soon Little Red Riding Hood arrived too.

261
00:22:52.260 --> 00:22:53.380
She knocked on the door.

262
00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:59.280
Instead of grandma, Little Red Riding Hood was met by a handsome wolf.

263
00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.880
Now they have a little Little Red Riding Hood too.

264
00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:05.480
They lived happily until the end of their days.

265
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.140
And that's the end of the story.

266
00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.360
This is my version.

267
00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:12.060
I believe you have your own too.

268
00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:16.040
See you in the next video.